  The Boolean domain is basically what George Boole built in 1847.
  It can express the logical operator AND with multiplication, and NOT with  (1 — x) .
  De Morgan, using his own notation, found OR.
  As in X or Y or both.
  X or Y but not both is called XOR (exclusive OR).
  How did he do?

  Let's define Z as everything that is neither Y nor X
  —— that is: { (NOT Y) AND (NOT X) }:

    z  =  (1 — y)(1 — x)

  Now the negation of Z is
  { everything that is either Y or X or both }
  (no extra case) ——
  —— which is what we're looking for.
  Let's call it W:

    W  =  1 — z  =  1 — (1 — y)(1 — x)

  W is:

    {
      everything that is not
      [
        (
          everything that is not Y
        )
        AND
        (
          everything that is not X
        )
      ]
    }

  Apparently this is equivalent to: W is Y or X...
  Funny how uncertainty becomes impractical
  in a language optimised for truth.
  Let's say Y is Farfisa Compact,
  then  1 — y  is everything that is not Farfisa Compact,
  including Vox Continental.
  But now X is Vox Continental,
  then  1 — x  is everything that is not Vox Continental.
  Now  (1 — y)(1 — x)  is the intersection of these two sets,
  so it can be anything
  as long as it's neither Farfisa Compact
  nor Vox Continental (nor both).
  So W is
  { NOT (NEITHER Farfisa Compact NOR Vox Continental NOR both) } .
  Negation of negation is affirmation,
  so  {NOT NEITHER} = {EITHER}
  and  {NOT NOR} = {OR} .
  So W is
  { EITHER Farfisa Compact  OR Vox Continental  OR both } .
  /*
    In this example, "both" is an empty set.
    An empty set is a set.
    Use e.g. mammal and able to fly (or swim or whatever)
    if you don't like empty sets.
  */

    w  =  1 — (1 — y)(1 — x)          //  This reads:
                                      //  { all X }  plus  { all Y }
       =  1 — (1 — x — y + xy)        //  minus  { all X that are Y }
                                      //  (or Y that are X),
       =  1 — 1 + x + y — xy          //
                                      //  xy is part of x and y
       =  x + y — xy                  //  ——> remove it once or get it twice.

  Now if O is Compact Organ,
  then all Y are O and all X are O;
  and we should find that all W are O too.

    xo  =  x
    yo  =  y
    wo  =  (x + y — xy)o  =  xo + yo — xyo
        =  x + y — xy  =  w

  Ok.
  Powerful, no doubt.
  But a bit convoluted.
  Modern Boolean algebra kept 0 and 1,
  but instead of everything and nothing,
  they now typically mean (respectively) false and true
  (although not necessarily).
  And the operators AND, NOT and OR
  are used explicitly or with the symbols:

    ∧ (AND)  ¬ (NOT)  ∨ (OR)

  These operators can be combined to obtain new ones
  like XOR, NOR, &c.

  Now how do we make a machine with this?
  It turns out that the dichotomy  0||1
  (or  false||true )
  has a counterpart in electronics.
  It's called a switch, or an interrupter.
  There's one on your phone.
  It has two positions:  off/on  or  open/closed .
      open:  disconnects the circuit.
    closed:  allows current to flow.

  It also turns out that most tubes and transistors
  can act as voltage-controlled,
  or current-controlled, interrupters.
  Also, as we've seen,
  capacitors can store voltage.
  So a network of transistors,
  each paired with a capacitor,
  can route and store voltage.
  The discharge of a capacitor is exponential,
  so zero is never actually reached.
  We need a threshold.
  Then a low enough voltage means 0,
  otherwise it's 1.
  Serial wiring gives AND,
  parallel wiring gives OR,
  and NOT is simply an inverter.
  When a transistor receives the prescribed current or voltage,
  it switches.
  And its capacitor charges or discharges accordingly.
  The electrical signal becomes a binary discrete signal.
  The manier instances of transistor/capacitor pairs,
  the more binary data.
  But the charge and the discharge of a capacitor
  are not instantaneous.
  So computing this data takes a non-negligible time
  (no more speed of light).
  (Keep that in mind.)
