  We now have a discrete input signal. We want to apply a filter to it.
  We have block diagrams to help us see equations as signal paths.
  Additions and multiplications are pretty straightforward.
  But there are integrators sometimes.

  We've seen (Chapters I & III)
  that integrating a function's rate of change
  rebuilds the function.
  But if we integrate the function itself,
  we get something else.
  (Antiderivative obviously, but that's not my target here.)

  /*
    In what follows I loosely use "function"
    to mean "function's output value".
    It's not semantically rigorous
    but it works around the confusion
    between function output and output signal.
    I.e.: if we integrate our input signal over time,
    the function's input (t) is time
    and its output ( x(t) ) is the input signal...
  */

  Think graphical.
  At each infinitesimal step,
  we multiply the function by a small increment in its variable (differential).
  On a graph, the differential is a horizontal (infinitesimal) segment;
  the function's value is a vertical segment.
  This figure is called a rectangle.
  And multiplying one side by the other
  is how we measure the area of a rectangle.

  So each step of an integral is the area of a rectangle.
  And the integration itself is the sum of all the rectangles.

  This produces an approximation of the area under the curve.
  And it's as precise as the differential is small.

  Except here our variable is time and our differential is T
  (the sampling period,
  the time interval between two consecutive samples).
  It's measurable and therefore not really infinitesimal.

  So the rectangle is a nice start,
  but it could easily benefit from some optimisation.

  What follows is very simple,
  on the sine qua non condition that you think graphical.
  Did I mention THINK GRAPHICAL?

  [Trapezoids]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/cv1da4kefc

  Between samples, information is lost.
  But we can do better than hard-stepped jumps:
  the shortest path between two points is a straight line.
  And two points are all a straight line requires to be defined.

  So, knowing the previous amplitude (which we do),
  we would better approximate the lost curve
  by drawing a straight line to the current amplitude.

  The figure which previously was a rectangle
  is now a right triangle on top of a rectangle.
  (If you cannot picture it, see last Desmos graph.)
