  //  Trapezoidal differentiator
  This piece of block diagram
  can be injected in bigger block diagrams
  by replacing [ 1/s ] elements.
  Fine.
  But what if we want to directly discretise a filter's transfer function?
  We need to replace s.

  Differentiation is the reciprocal to integration.
  So we need the reciprocal to a trapezoidal integrator.
  A function maps x to y.
  Its reciprocal maps y to x.
  So we need to treat x as the unknown
  and express it in terms of y.

  I chose this method because I find it easier.
  But reading the block diagram backwards
  is as valid a solution.

             T     1 + 1/z                     T     1 + 1/z
    H(z)  = ——— · —————————    <=>    y  =  x ——— · —————————
             2     1 — 1/z                     2     1 — 1/z

              2         1 + 1/z                     2     1 — 1/z
    <=>    y ———  =  x —————————    <=>    x  =  y ——— · —————————
              T         1 — 1/z                     T     1 + 1/z

  Ok, so the reciprocal is the inverse.
  Laplace's magic is still operating.
  Good.

  Then, if  H_a(s)  is an analogue filter's transfer function,
  and  H_d(z)  is its trapezoidal discretised counterpart,
  the substitution:

    H_a(s)  <——  H_d(z)

  requires the substitution:

             2     1 — 1/z
    s  <——  ——— · —————————
             T     1 + 1/z

  Our trapezoidal differentiator is a ratio:

     2     1 — 1/z       2 — 2/z       2z — 2
    ——— · —————————  =  —————————  =  ————————
     T     1 + 1/z       T + T/z       Tz + T

    The numerator is:  2z — 2
  The denominator is:  Tz + T

  They're both affine functions of z.
  Affine functions are linear.
  So a trapezoidal differentiator
  is a ratio of linear functions of z.

  Hence this transformation being commonly called
  BILINEAR TRANSFORM (BLT).

