#09  Is the bilinear transform stable?
===================================================================================================

  Yes, no, maybe... we don't know.
  The analogue prototype is stable
  when all its poles have negative real parts.
  A pole is a root of the denominator,
  which is a polynomial in s.
  So a pole p is a value of  s  =  σ + jω .
  So  Re(p) < 0  means  σ < 0 .
  Since  z = exp(sT) = exp(σT + jωT) = exp(σT) exp(jωT) ,
  |z| = exp(σT)  and  arg(z) = ωT  (Chapter I).
  And since T is always positive
  and  exp(x)  is strictly positive and strictly increasing
  for all real x:

    σ < 0    <=>   σT < 0

    <=>    exp(σT) < 1

    <=>    |z| < 1

  According to the stinky-self-quote theorem,
  the Working-class BLT gives:

                         2     1 — 1/z
    BLT{s}  =  S(z)  =  ——— · —————————
                         T     1 + 1/z

  We want to know whether:

    Re(s) < 0    <=>    Re( S(z) ) < 0

  or, equivalently, whether:

    Re( S(z) ) < 0    <=>    |z| < 1

  Go:

     T            z — 1             T
    ——— S(z)  =  ———————    <=>    ——— S(z)(1 + z)  =  z — 1
     2            z + 1             2

            T          T
    <=>    ——— S(z) + ——— S(z)·z + 1 — z  =  0
            2          2

            T                    T
    <=>    ——— S(z) + 1  =  z — ——— z
            2                    2

    <=>    1 + TS(z)/2  =  z( 1 — TS(z)/2 )

                   1 + TS(z)/2       2 + TS(z)
    <=>    z  =  ——————————————  =  ——————————
                   1 — TS(z)/2       2 — TS(z)

  /*
    //  Another brick in the 4th wall
      That's just  BLT⁻¹{ S(z) } ...
      One more shortcut we didn't take.
      Reasons:
        #  I wrote the draft for this section
           long before I decided to patent BLT
           under the WTF public license;
        #  I cannot remember if I purposely derived the inverse BLT
           or if it was a happy accident...
           Considering my incurable propension to over-explain
           each semicolon with several levels of nested comments
           to ensure all the questions no one will ever ask get answered...
           My bet is on the happy accident...
        #  Don't read what precedes, it's boring.
  */

  Let's define:
    #  σ₂  =  Re( S(z) )
    #  ω₂  =  Im( S(z) )

           2/T + ( σ₂ + jω₂ )       2/T + σ₂ + jω₂
    z  =  ————————————————————  =  ————————————————
           2/T — ( σ₂ + jω₂ )       2/T — σ₂ — jω₂

                   │  2/T + σ₂ + jω₂  │      | 2/T + σ₂ + jω₂ |
    <=>    |z|  =  │ ———————————————— │  =  ————————————————————
                   │  2/T — σ₂ — jω₂  │      | 2/T — σ₂ — jω₂ |
  /*
    Absolute values are real and positive,
    so |z| and |z|² have the same sign
    and the same lack of imaginary part;
    ergo: no need to bother ourselves with a gigantic square root.
  */
                     ( 2/T + σ₂ )² + ( +ω₂ )²
    <=>    |z|²  =  —————————————————————————
                     ( 2/T — σ₂ )² + ( —ω₂ )²

                     4/T² + 4σ₂/T + σ₂² + ω₂²
    <=>    |z|²  =  —————————————————————————
                     4/T² — 4σ₂/T + σ₂² + ω₂²
  /*
    We want the square root of this expression
    to be less than one.
    So we want this expression to be less than one.
    So we want the numerator inferior to the denominator.
  */

    4/T² + 4σ₂/T + σ₂² + ω₂²  <  4/T² — 4σ₂/T + σ₂² + ω₂²

    //  (Subtract redundant terms from both sides)

    <=>   +4σ₂/T  <  —4σ₂/T    //  (Divide both sides by T/4)

    <=>    +σ₂  <  —σ₂    <=>    σ₂ + σ₂  <  0

    <=>    2σ₂  <  0    <=>    σ₂  <  0/2  =  0

  Notice that this demonstration survives
  if you flip the inequality
  or replace it with an equality.
  Therefore:
    #  Re(s) < 0    <=>    Re(s_BLT) < 0
    #  Re(s) > 0    <=>    Re(s_BLT) > 0
    #  Re(s) = 0    <=>    Re(s_BLT) = 0

  So: is the bilinear transform stable?
  Yes, no, maybe. Depends on the analogue prototype.
  Bilinear transform preserves stability, instability and marginal stability.
  All this from failing at drawing a proper trapeze...

  Jolly good, jolly good.
  But what about cutoff pre-warping?
  Moving the cutoff moves the poles.
  Indeed.
  But if your cutoff is strictly positive,
  there's nothing  tan(x)  can do about it.

  Unless  x ≥ π/2 ...

    ωc·T/2  ≥  π/2    <=>    2π·fc/2·f_s  ≥  π/2

    <=>    2·fc/f_s  ≥  1    <=>    fc  ≥  f_s/2  =  Nyquist

  Basically: do not try to filter out frequencies that are not there.
  (Unless you seek chaos of course.)

  And what if there's no analogue prototype?
  Then I will slowly pronounce:
  bi_li_ne_ar _ TRA_NS_FO_RM
  (emphasis on "transform").
  In that case, your starting point should likely be a function of z.
  Then check  |z| < 1  and you're good ——
  —— that is if you want stability,
  otherwise just flip the inequality.



